@lang en
IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `usca-interpretation-time`

-- MEANT TO FAIL.  Every assertion in this file states an answer that a plausible, naive reading gives and that the deposited text does not
-- support.  Each is expected to print `assertion failed`, and check.sh counts them (expected_failed in check.sh, expected_red in encoding.json).
-- If one of them ever starts to pass, either the module has stopped following the text or the text has changed: read the diagnostic.
-- Each expected value below was computed from the naive rule it names, not from the module.

GIVEN d IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Sundays only` d MEANS `is a Sunday` d

GIVEN d IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Saturdays, Sundays and Monday 16 March 2026` d MEANS `is a Saturday` d OR `is a Sunday` d OR d EQUALS YMD 2026 3 16

-- Naive 1: a year is 365 days, so three years from 16 July 2025 end 1095 days on, 15 July 2028.  Gov. Code § 6803 counts the added day of a leap
-- year and the day before it as one, and 29 February 2028 is in the period, so the text gives 16 July 2028.
#ASSERT `before the rest-day rule, the last day of` 3 `years counted from` (YMD 2025 7 16) EQUALS YMD 2028 7 15

-- Naive 2: five years from 15 January 2025 end 1825 days on, 14 January 2030.  The text gives 15 January 2030 (the leap day of 2028 is in the period).
#ASSERT `before the rest-day rule, the last day of` 5 `years counted from` (YMD 2025 1 15) EQUALS YMD 2030 1 14

-- Naive 3: five years from 1 September 2025 end 1825 days on, 31 August 2030.  The text gives 1 September 2030.
#ASSERT `before the rest-day rule, the last day of` 5 `years counted from` (YMD 2025 9 1) EQUALS YMD 2030 8 31

-- Naive 4: the first day counts, so ten days from 10 March 2026 end on 19 March.  Gov. Code § 6800 excludes the first day: the text gives 20 March.
#ASSERT `before the rest-day rule, the last day of` 10 `days counted from` (YMD 2026 3 10) EQUALS YMD 2026 3 19

-- Naive 5: Saturday is a rest day under every list, so thirty days from 1 January 2026 move off Saturday 31 January to Monday 2 February under the
-- Government Code's list.  Gov. Code § 6700 lists Sundays and named days, not Saturdays: the Government Code list gives 31 January.
#ASSERT `the last day of a period of` 30 days `counted from` (YMD 2026 1 1) `under the calendar` `Sundays only` EQUALS YMD 2026 2 2

-- Naive 6: the one-step exclusion of a holiday last day and the run to the next day that is not a holiday are the same rule.  They differ when the day
-- after the holiday last day is a holiday too.  Last day Saturday 14 March 2026, with Sunday 15 March and Monday 16 March also holidays: excluding it once
-- gives Sunday 15 March; Code Civ. Proc. § 12a(a) ("to and including the next day that is not a holiday") gives Tuesday 17 March.
-- This asserts the run answer from the one-step function, and fails.
#ASSERT `with a holiday last day also excluded, once` `Saturdays, Sundays and Monday 16 March 2026` (YMD 2026 3 14) EQUALS YMD 2026 3 17

-- Naive 7: a statute enacted on 10 March 2026 at a regular session takes effect 90 days later, 8 June 2026.  Gov. Code § 9600(a) says January 1 next
-- following the 90-day period, 1 January 2027.
#ASSERT `the day a regular-session statute goes into effect when enacted on` (YMD 2026 3 10) EQUALS YMD 2026 6 8

-- Naive 8: daylight saving time begins at the next midnight, so it is not in effect at 3 a.m. on the second Sunday of March (8 March 2026).  Gov. Code § 6808(b)
-- has the period commence at 2 a.m. that day, so at 3 a.m. it is in effect.
#ASSERT NOT `daylight saving time is in effect at the clock reading` (Moment WITH `the day` IS YMD 2026 3 8, `seconds after midnight` IS 10800)
